        "psychoeducation": "Why does recognising risk as a parent matter? Children learn through repetition, safety, and\nconnection. When adults respond in calm, predictable ways, the child's nervous\nsystem gradually learns that the world is safer and more manageable. Over time,\nthis builds self-regulation, confidence, and trust. When recognising risk as a parent is missing,\nchildren may rely more on survival behaviours such as shutting down, exploding,\nor clinging because they do not yet have another way to cope.",
        "learningGoals": [
          "Understand how recognising risk as a parent supports healthy brain and emotional development.",
          "Identify common triggers and patterns where recognising risk as a parent is most needed.",
          "Practice simple, repeatable responses that feel realistic in daily family life.",
          "Strengthen the parent-child relationship through calm, consistent interactions."
        ],
        "stepByStep": [
          "Pause: Notice your own body first - breathing, tension, tone of voice.",
          "Name: Briefly name what is happening for the child (for example: 'This is really hard for you right now').",
          "Connect: Move closer if safe, soften your tone, and show you are staying with them.",
          "Guide: Offer one simple, doable next step instead of a lecture or long explanation.",
          "Repeat: Use the same calm script and steps across similar situations to build predictability."
        ],
        "parentScripts": [
          "I can see this feels really big for you. I'm not angry - you're not in trouble. Let's slow down together.",
          "Your feelings are okay. It's what we do with them that we're practicing.",
          "I'm going to stay close while you calm down. When you're ready, we'll work it out together.",
          "We can try that again. Everyone gets another chance to practice."
        ],
        "practicalExamples": [
          "A morning routine where everything is rushed and the child refuses to get dressed. The parent uses recognising risk as a parent by slowing their own pace, offering two simple choices, and staying calm when the child protests.",
          "A homework battle where the child shuts down. Instead of pushing harder, the parent validates the struggle, breaks the task into tiny steps, and celebrates effort rather than perfection.",
          "A public meltdown in a supermarket. The parent focuses on safety, keeps their voice low, moves to a quieter space if possible, and later reflects on triggers and what might help next time."
        ],
        "reflectionQuestions": [
          "When my child is struggling, what usually happens inside me first - do I speed up, shut down, or get louder?",
          "What is one phrase I could practice saying to myself before I respond to my child?",
          "Where in our daily routine does recognising risk as a parent feel hardest to use (mornings, bedtimes, transitions)?",
          "Who could I talk to or debrief with after a hard parenting moment so I do not carry it alone?"
        ],
        "practiceIdeas": [
          "Choose one daily routine such as bedtime and focus on using the same calm script for one week.",
          "Write down two or three phrases that help you stay grounded and keep them on your phone or fridge.",
          "After a tough moment, jot a quick note: what happened, what you tried, and one thing you might do differently next time.",
          "Share one small win with a trusted friend or worker each week to reinforce progress rather than perfection."
        ],
        "closingMessage": "You do not have to get recognising risk as a parent perfect for it to work. Children benefit from\n'good enough' parenting where adults keep coming back, repairing after mistakes,\nand trying again. Every time you pause, notice, and respond with a little more\ncalm and connection, you are already changing the story for your child.",
        "simplifiedParentVersion": "What this skill means:\nrecognising risk as a parent is about helping you and your child stay calm, understand each other, and get through tricky moments together.\n\nWhy it matters:\nWhen you use recognising risk as a parent, your child feels safer, more understood, and more connected to you. This makes hard moments easier for both of you.\n\nHow to use it:\n- Keep your voice calm and steady.\n- Use short, simple sentences.\n- Show your child you are listening.\n- Give clear choices when things feel stuck.\n- Take a slow breath before responding.\n\nExample:\nIf your child is upset, you might say:\n\"I'm here. Let's take a breath. We can figure this out together.\"\n\nTry this today:\nPick one small moment — like getting ready, bedtime, or a small disagreement — and practice using recognising risk as a parent in a calm, simple way.",
        "safetyRedFlags": [
          "The child appears fearful, withdrawn, or hypervigilant during discussions related to recognising risk as a parent.",
          "The parent becomes overwhelmed, dissociative, or unable to remain emotionally regulated.",
          "There is evidence of escalating conflict, shouting, or intimidation in the home.",
          "The child reports feeling unsafe, scared, or threatened.",
          "The parent expresses hopelessness, extreme frustration, or thoughts of harming themselves or others.",
          "There are signs of substance use impacting safety or supervision.",
          "The parent or child discloses past or current abuse, neglect, or violence."
        ],
        "safetyEscalation": [
          "If the parent or child expresses feeling unsafe or at risk.",
          "If conflict escalates beyond verbal disagreement and becomes threatening.",
          "If the parent is unable to regulate enough to continue practicing recognising risk as a parent safely.",
          "If the child shows signs of trauma activation that require clinical support.",
          "If there is any disclosure of abuse, neglect, or violence.",
          "If the worker feels unsure, overwhelmed, or out of scope.",
          "If mandatory reporting thresholds are met or suspected."
        ]
